@page "/"

<PageTitle>Duniverse</PageTitle>

<div class="hero" style="background-image: linear-gradient(rgba(23, 19, 16, 0.82), rgba(23, 19, 16, 0.88)), url('/images/main-menu-background.jpg');">
    <h1>Welcome to the Duniverse</h1>
    <p class="tagline">
        An unofficial encyclopedia of Frank Herbert's Dune, a story of desert planets,
        prophecy, empire, and ecology that changed what science fiction was allowed to be.
    </p>

<section class="home-section">
    <h2>Why Dune Matters</h2>
    <p>
        Published in 1965, <em>Dune</em> ranks among the most influential science fiction
        novels ever written. Frank Herbert skipped the ray guns and rocket derring-do; he
        built a story around ecology, religion, resource scarcity, and the perils of
        charismatic leadership. A desert planet holds the empire's only source of the spice
        melange, and that single fact drags every noble house, religious order, and imperial
        schemer into its orbit. Herbert's premise, that one resource could dictate the fate
        of civilizations, handed science fiction a new kind of epic: one built on environments
        and power, not just heroes squaring off against villains.
    </p>
    <p>
        Its influence runs through the science fiction that came after it. Critics have long
        drawn lines between Arrakis and Tatooine, and between Paul Atreides' ascent and the
        "chosen one" arc George Lucas later gave Luke Skywalker in <em>Star Wars</em>. Dune
        shows up again and again on lists of the works that shaped the modern space epic.
        Herbert cared less about creating a messiah, though, than about warning readers away
        from worshipping one: Paul's arc follows the shape of a hero's journey on purpose,
        engineered to leave readers uneasy about the charismatic figures they're tempted to
        idolize.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
    <h2>About Frank Herbert</h2>
    <p>
        Frank Herbert (1920-1986) worked as an American journalist before he became one of
        science fiction's defining authors. Years spent as a newspaper reporter and editor
        left their mark on <em>Dune</em>: the novel's dense, research-driven world-building
        reads like the work of someone who spent a career chasing down facts before writing
        a sentence. Herbert went on to write five sequels himself (<em>Dune Messiah</em>,
        <em>Children of Dune</em>, <em>God Emperor of Dune</em>, <em>Heretics of Dune</em>,
        and <em>Chapterhouse: Dune</em>), stretching the saga across ten thousand years of
        future history before he died in 1986.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
    <h2>About the creation of Dune</h2>
    <p>
        The seed of <em>Dune</em> was, quite literally, sand. In the late 1950s, Herbert set
        out to write a magazine article about a U.S. Department of Agriculture project using
        grasses to hold back the shifting sand dunes along the Oregon coast. He never finished
        that article. The research stayed with him anyway: the notion of sand as a force
        capable of swallowing roads, towns, and rivers took root, and over the next six years
        it grew into a whole desert planet, complete with its own ecology, its own religion,
        and a people who had learned to survive on it.
    </p>
    <p>
        The finished novel ran first as a serial in <em>Analog Science Fiction</em> magazine.
        Roughly twenty publishers rejected it afterward, put off by its length and its strangeness
        for the market at the time. It finally found a home in 1965 with Chilton Books, a
        publisher better known for auto repair manuals. <em>Dune</em> went on to win the Hugo
        and the inaugural Nebula Award, and it remains one of the best-selling science fiction
        novels of all time.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
    <h2>In Frank Herbert's Own Words</h2>
    <p>
        Herbert spoke and wrote at length, beyond the novel itself, about the dangers he saw
        in charismatic leaders, unchecked power, and technology deployed without a second
        thought: the same warnings <em>Dune</em> was built to dramatize.
    </p>
    <div class="quote-grid">
        @foreach (var quote in AuthorQuotes)
        {
            <div class="quote-card">
                <p class="quote-text">"@quote.Text"</p>
                <span class="quote-author">- @quote.Source</span>
            </div>
        }
    </div>
</section>

</div>

@code {
    private record AuthorQuote(string Text, string Source);

    private static readonly List<AuthorQuote> AuthorQuotes = new()
    {
        new("Don't give over all of your critical faculties to people in power, no matter how admirable those people may appear to be.", "Frank Herbert in Dune Genesis, Omni magazine, 1980"),
        new("People tend to give over every decision-making capacity to any leader who can wrap himself in the myth fabric of the society.", "Frank Herbert in Dune Genesis, Omni magazine, 1980"),
        new("Dune was aimed at this whole idea of the infallible leader because my view of history says mistakes made by a leader (or made in a leader's name) are amplified by the numbers who follow without question.", "Frank Herbert in Dune Genesis, Omni magazine, 1980"),
        new("Heroes are painful, superheroes are a catastrophe. The mistakes of superheroes involve too many of us in disaster.", "Frank Herbert in Dune Genesis, Omni magazine, 1980"),
        new("It is demonstrable that power structures tend to attract people who want power for power's sake.", "Frank Herbert in Dune Genesis, Omni magazine, 1980"),
        new("Technology is both a tool for helping humans and for destroying them. This is the paradox of our times which we're compelled to face.", "Frank Herbert in Conversations in Port Townsend, interview with Tim O'Reilly, 1983"),
        new("By the time we awaken faintly to the awareness that we have been socially conditioned, we find ourselves so indoctrinated that it's difficult, if not impossible, to break the old patterns.", "Frank Herbert in Science Fiction: Today and Tomorrow Magazine, 1974"),
    };
}
